<<<
poverty) (poorness. The dominant sense of poor is having little money
===
**poverty) (poorness.** The dominant sense of *poor* is having little money
>>>

<<<
The noun corresponding to this dominant sense is poverty, & poorness is never so used in modern English.
===
The noun corresponding to this dominant sense is *poverty*, & *poorness* is never so used in modern English.
>>>

<<<
the more does poverty give way to poorness—Poverty is no excuse for theft; The poverty (or poorness) of the soil; The poorness (or poverty) of the harvest; The poorness of his performance. See -TY & -NESS.
===
the more does *poverty* give way to *poorness*—*Poverty is no excuse for theft; The poverty (or poorness) of the soil; The poorness (or poverty) of the harvest; The poorness of his performance.* See ^^-TY^^ & ^^-NESS^^.
>>>

